<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Frederick, Grand Duke of Baden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> ruled for 73 years, first as Margrave of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> from 1738. In 1771 he inherited Baden-Baden and reunited the margraviate. An enlightened ruler, he abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1783. Siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, he gained large territories and became Elector in 1803 and Grand Duke in 1806, making Baden a major state of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-frederick-grand-duke-of-baden.jpg" alt="Charles Frederick, Grand Duke of Baden" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Friedrich_Markgraf_von_Baden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Frederick was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> on 22 November 1728, the son of Hereditary Prince Frederick of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> and Amalia of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Dietz</a>. He succeeded his grandfather as Margrave of Baden-Durlach in 1738 and ruled personally from 1746. On 28 January 1751 he married Caroline Louise of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, and they had five children, of whom three survived infancy. In 1747 he was elected a fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1771 Charles Frederick inherited the Margraviate of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> from the Catholic line of his family, which reunited the original land of Baden. It made him the Protestant ruler of a state that was overwhelmingly Catholic, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> allowed it, because the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> had converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and had been permitted to keep control of the Protestant body of the Diet.</p><p>He was regarded as a good example of an enlightened despot, supporting schools, universities, jurisprudence, the civil service, the economy, culture and urban development. He outlawed torture in 1767 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1783. With his architect Friedrich Weinbrenner he built the classical buildings that distinguish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>.</p><p>In 1803 he became Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and in 1806 the first Grand Duke. Through the policy of his minister Sigismund von Reitzenstein, Baden acquired the Bishopric of Constance, the right-bank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> territories of the bishoprics of Basel, Strassburg and Speyer, and also the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ortenau">Ortenau</a>. In 1806 Baden joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles Frederick died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> on 10 June 1811, one of the few German rulers to die during the Napoleonic era. His grandson Charles succeeded him. In 1787 he had made a second, morganatic marriage to Louise Caroline Geyer von Geyersberg, whose children could not succeed. By 1817 his descendants by the first marriage were dying out, and to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> from passing to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, Grand Duke Charles changed the succession law to give the Hochberg family full rights. The constitution of 1818 and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> of 1819 confirmed this, and the descendants of his son Leopold ruled Baden until 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Frederick, Grand Duke of Baden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Margrave <b>Charles Frederick</b> had united the two Baden margraviates in 1771.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-baden">Electorate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>Elector <b>Charles Frederick</b> of Baden joined the Confederation of the Rhine in 1806 and became grand duke, gaining Austrian Breisgau, mediatised lands and the Lake Constance region.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Frederick</b>, an enlightened ruler, abolished serfdom in 1783.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>When Margrave August George died without heirs in 1771, his lands passed to <b>Charles Frederick</b> of the Margraviate of Baden-Durlach, reuniting Baden, which became the Grand Duchy of Baden in 1806.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-baden">Margraviate of Baden-Baden</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Frederick</b>, ruling from 1738, was a model enlightened prince who abolished serfdom in 1783.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Margraviate of Baden-Durlach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,8.4&amp;year=1781" aria-label="Karlsruhe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles Frederick, Grand Duke of Baden"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5469 5904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5469 5904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,8.4&amp;year=1781">What was here in 1781? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
